{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nSibling epic `wide-wimpy-insect` (87cb-7121-a3b6-4606, Track I — reduce defect introduction)\ncommissioned a sweep for hand-maintained representation mirrors: one canonical fact with two\nmasters that can drift apart. Representation drift is the dominant sub-mechanism of the\nproduct-logic defect category in the R2S1 mechanism read.\n\nThe sweep is complete and its inventory is committed at `reports/stability/mirror-inventory.md`\n(task `yogic-soapstone-takin`, f9b9-83ad-931c-4833). It examined **77 candidate sets** across five\nareas and found **14 genuine mirrors, 5 already drifted**. That task's deliverable was the\ninventory; the remediation is this epic.\n\n## Scope\n\nOne child per genuine mirror, each proposing a named fix pattern — generate-from-canonical,\nadd-parity-check, add-census-assertion-to-existing-test, or add-route-flag. Each child carries its\nown two-master `file:line` evidence and a severity, and links `discovered_from` the sweep task.\n\nRecommended order (highest value first):\n\n1. **F1** — reducer event-type dispatch has two masters. Not yet drifted, but a known type with no\n   handler is folded into a snapshot and its file retired: silent permanent data loss.\n2. **F12** — `docs/exit-codes.md` is drifted from `ROUTES` today, in both directions, while\n   claiming to be pinned by a test whose entire assertion is one string check.\n3. **F13** — `_validate_gate_step_ids` already exists and is called for plan-review only; the same\n   silent-degradation class is left open for code-review and completion-verification. NOT one line\n   each: the validator's signature is `(doc, required, *, gate_name)`, and the only `required` set\n   that exists is `_PLAN_REVIEW_REQUIRED_STEP_IDS`. So each remaining gate needs (a) its OWN\n   required-step-id frozenset, naming the step ids that gate's dispatcher logic actually\n   references — code-review reuses `STEP_VERIFY`/`STEP_DECIDE` plus its finalization cluster, and\n   completion-verification has its own — and (b) a call sited right after that gate's\n   `_gate_doc(...)` load, mirroring the plan-review call. The child must DERIVE each set from the\n   dispatcher's real references rather than copying plan-review's, or the check passes\n   vacuously while still permitting the rename it exists to catch.\n\nThen the remaining HIGH findings (F5, F3), the MEDIUM ones (F4, F10, F2, F6, F7), and the LOW ones\n(F8, F9, F11, F14).\n\n## Method note carried forward\n\nThe sweep's most reliable discriminator, worth applying to any future audit: **a comment claiming\n\"single-sourced\" or \"kept in lockstep\" is a marker for an UNENFORCED mirror.** All four such claims\nin the tree were false. Equally: equal counts hide drift — F12's two sides both had 78 rows and\ndifferent members, so only a set difference found it.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [x] AC1: every child is closed, each having either generated the mirrored value from its canonical\n      source or added a gate that fails on divergence. Verified by each child's own close.\n- [x] AC2: no fix collapses a DISTINCT CONCEPT. The inventory's rejected list records nine candidate\n      families that look duplicated but are semantically different predicates; a remediation that\n      merges one of those introduces a defect. Verified by review of each child's change against the\n      inventory's rejection rationale."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788422254919250001,"uuid":"a496cbd9-8f89-4349-b972-eee09700a914"}